1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:08,782
Every day our teams route tickets, tag documents and triage alerts, and many now send each decision to a frontier language model.

2
00:00:08,782 --> 00:00:15,062
Here's when a small trained classifier does that job better: cheaper, faster, and inside our walls.

3
00:00:16,262 --> 00:00:23,834
Classification at volume is millions of short, repetitive decisions with fixed labels and plenty of past examples.

4
00:00:23,834 --> 00:00:28,218
What matters is speed, cost per decision, privacy and consistency.

5
00:00:28,218 --> 00:00:35,125
A frontier model is a brilliant generalist, but we pay per token, and every request leaves the building.

6
00:00:36,325 --> 00:00:37,806
CLM splits the work.

7
00:00:37,806 --> 00:00:41,778
An NVIDIA Nemotron model reads each text and never changes.

8
00:00:41,778 --> 00:00:47,769
A small model on top learns our labels from our own examples, in minutes, from a browser.

9
00:00:47,769 --> 00:00:52,818
We get a probability for every label, in milliseconds, on our own hardware.

10
00:00:54,018 --> 00:01:02,179
On a ticket-routing demo, a model trained on three hundred examples gets eighty-eight to ninety-six percent of unseen tickets right.

11
00:01:02,179 --> 00:01:05,580
Untrained, the same reader manages fifty-seven percent.

12
00:01:05,580 --> 00:01:12,567
The demo data is synthetic, so the real test is our own data, against a frontier model, with the included script.

13
00:01:13,767 --> 00:01:20,686
It's fast: a median of thirty-three milliseconds on one laptop, with no network hop, queue or rate limit.

14
00:01:20,686 --> 00:01:26,089
One laptop handles about thirty-six answers a second, roughly three million a day.

15
00:01:27,289 --> 00:01:28,248
And it's cheap.

16
00:01:28,248 --> 00:01:33,551
A million decisions take about eight hours of laptop compute, around eight dollars.

17
00:01:33,551 --> 00:01:42,689
At list prices, the same million tickets cost about fifty-eight dollars on the smallest frontier model, and over a thousand on the larger ones.

18
00:01:42,689 --> 00:01:45,628
Conservative estimates, for the frontier side.

19
00:01:46,828 --> 00:01:47,728
It stays ours.

20
00:01:47,728 --> 00:01:58,016
Texts never leave our machines, the same input gives the same answer, and model files are versioned and verified, with a full governance pack in the repository.

21
00:01:59,216 --> 00:02:01,350
Use each where it's strongest.

22
00:02:01,350 --> 00:02:05,833
CLM for fixed labels, high volume, and fast, private answers.

23
00:02:05,833 --> 00:02:10,031
A frontier model for open questions, writing and reasoning.

24
00:02:10,031 --> 00:02:16,649
Best of both: CLM decides, and only its uncertain cases go to a frontier model or a person.

25
00:02:17,849 --> 00:02:25,462
Getting started is small: a few hundred labelled examples, minutes of training in a browser, and a laptop or small server.

26
00:02:25,462 --> 00:02:33,263
Before real decisions, people check the labels, the probabilities are calibrated, and the governance checklist is signed off.

27
00:02:34,463 --> 00:02:49,334
We recommend a four-week pilot on one high-volume workflow: label five hundred real examples, test CLM and a frontier model side by side with the included script, and decide on measured accuracy, cost and latency.

28
00:02:49,334 --> 00:02:50,026
Thank you.
